==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|overdriven}}, from {{inh|en|ang|oferdrīfan}}, equivalent to {{af|en|over-|drive}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|uurdrieuwe|t=to overdo, overstate}}, {{cog|nl|overdrijven|t=to exaggerate}}, {{cog|nds-de|overdrieven}}, {{m|nds-de|överdrieven|t=to overdo, exaggerate}}, {{cog|de|übertreiben|t=to overdo, exaggerate}}, {{cog|no|overdrive|t=to exaggerate}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|overdrives|overdriving|overdrove|overdriven}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[drive]] too hard, or far, or beyond strength.

===Etymology 2===
From {{af|en|over-|drive}} From the gear ''over'' "D" (drive) in an automatic transmission vehicle.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|dated}} A [[gear]], on an automobile, higher than the normal top gear.
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=Popular Mechanics|page=201|date=Oct|month=Oct|volume=156|number=4|passage=Cruising in '''overdrive''' at legal highway speed keeps rpm right at 1800, depending on rear-axle ratio.}}
# A state of heightened activity.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The ground beneath her feet|page=78|author=[[w:Salman Rushdie|Salman Rushdie]]|year=2000|passage=It is true, though it's got nothing to do with me, that the building boom that created the Bombay of my childhood went into '''overdrive''' in the years before my birth}}

